  "passages": [
    {
      "id": "pcp-short-abstract",
      "section": "Abstract",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "The PCP theorem gives every language in NP a polynomial-time verifier with logarithmic randomness, constantly many proof-bit queries, perfect completeness and soundness error at most one half.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.np_subset_pcp"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "Abstract",
        "block": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-short-theorem",
      "section": "1",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "If $x\\in\\mathcal L$, there is one proof accepted for every random seed. If $x\\notin\\mathcal L$, every proof is accepted with probability at most $1/2$.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.np_subset_pcp"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "1. Introduction",
        "block": 4
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-short-colour-constraints",
      "section": "2",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "**Lemma 2.1 (colouring constraints).** The restriction $P|_{H^m}$ is a proper colouring of the padded graph exactly when $A_P|_{H^m}=0$ and $B_P|_{H^{2m}}=0$, and $Q_P,A_P,B_P$ have degrees at most $d_0,3d_0,5d_0$.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.ThreeColorGrid.colorable_iff_exists_constraints"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "2. Preliminaries",
        "block": 10
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-short-weighted-test",
      "section": "4",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "If (10) fails on a fraction $\\delta<1/[2(c+2)^2]$ of pairs $(u,v)$, then $f$ is $2\\delta$-close to such a polynomial",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.GeneratorLowDegreeTest.exists_close"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "4. Low-degree tests over the binary alphabet",
        "block": 7
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-short-binary-decoding",
      "section": "4",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "If $q>2c$ and the test rejects with probability $\\kappa$, some polynomial $U$ of total degree at most $c$ satisfies",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.BinaryLocalRowDecoding.exists_polynomial_distance_le"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "4. Low-degree tests over the binary alphabet",
        "block": 16
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-short-outer-decoding",
      "section": "4",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "If this test rejects $(f,f')$ with probability $\\kappa$ and $J\\kappa\\le1/(4\\cdot10^6)$, then some polynomial $P$ of total degree at most $D$ has $\\Delta_{\\rm full}(f,\\mathcal H_r(P))\\le S\\kappa$ and $\\Delta_{\\rm hom}(f,\\mathcal H_r(P))\\le S\\kappa$.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.EncodedLineLowDegreeTest.exists_close"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "4. Low-degree tests over the binary alphabet",
        "block": 30
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-short-outer-correction",
      "section": "5",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "If the local corrector rejects at $a$ with probability $\\kappa_a$, the probability over all its randomness, $L$ included, of rejection or a returned bit other than $L(P(a))$ satisfies",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.BinaryLocalCorrection.failure_le"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "5. Local correction and the zero-on-grid test",
        "block": 4
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-short-zero",
      "section": "5",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "If $A$ does not vanish on $H^k$ and the test rejects with probability $\\kappa$, then (15) holds.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.BinaryZeroOnGridCorrection.rejectionRate_lower_bound"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "5. Local correction and the zero-on-grid test",
        "block": 13
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "linked-source-12",
      "section": "6",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "For completeness, a proper colouring, its low-degree extension, exact vanishing certificates and honest lines tables, with degrees below $b^c$, pass every check for every seed: one fixed eight-table proof with perfect completeness.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.ThreeColorVerifier.exists_proof_test_eq_true"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "6. Soundness of the verifier",
        "block": 13
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "linked-source-13",
      "section": "7",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "After fair-bit sampling the rejection gap is at least $g_c/16$, and constantly many independent repetitions on the same proof reduce soundness error to $1/2$, keeping logarithmic randomness and constant queries.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.ThreeColorAmplifiedVerifier.verification_guarantees"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "7. Resources, implementation and the reverse inclusion",
        "block": 10
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-short-complexity",
      "section": "7",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "Lee's implementation finds the field, generators and cube roots by bounded search in time polynomial in $q$, hence in the input length, and compiles the verifier into Mathlib's finite multi-stack machine model with a polynomial cost bound.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Lang.ThreeColorRuntime.polyTimeAlg"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "7. Resources, implementation and the reverse inclusion",
        "block": 13
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "linked-source-14",
      "section": "7",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "For the converse, take the verifier and bounds $p,\\alpha,\\beta$ of Theorem 1.1, guess the first $p(n)$ proof bits and simulate the verifier, in polynomial time, on each of the at most $2^\\beta(2(n+2))^\\alpha$ random strings. Perfect completeness gives a prefix accepted on every run. If such a prefix existed for a no-instance, its zero extension would have acceptance probability one, contradicting soundness. The resulting witness relation is decidable in polynomial time, so the language lies in NP.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.pcp_subset_np_concrete"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "7. Resources, implementation and the reverse inclusion",
        "block": 17
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-short-derived-zero",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "$$\n\\frac{1-Dh/q}{2}\n\\le(1+2C)(\\kappa+2\\epsilon)+2\\delta_M+\\frac{D}{q-1}.\n\\tag{16}\n$$",
      "lean": [
